Yes, they seem to be easy. Letting aside any mis-/interpretation on
top, I can inspect a large sample data base (like the NAO data) to
back that up. In my own case (as of Jan 2011) I had (asc. vs. died)
8 vs. 4 human and 7 vs 1 dwarven valkyries. So while you're certainly
right if you point out the race advantage they also seem to be easier
than other roles. I think other attributes of the role also matter;
like HPs, Constitution, starting equipment (shield!), Strength, etc.

>
> Tourists for example aren't difficult because they are tourists. They
> are difficult because they are humans. (Yes, I know that tourists can
> only be humans.) Think about it.

Personally I prefer humans compared to, say, elves. (That preference
is certainly also dependent on other factors.) But tourists also start
without armor and with stats that seem not especially good to survive.

> Consider a theoretical "Gno Tou" with
> infravision who kills monsters by throwing his +2 darts from afar and
> who enjoys peaceful mines. Still that difficult?

I understand your point. Tourists could, in your hypothetical thought,
then (as Gnomes) be easier than they currently are. The other factors
that are associated with tourists, e.g. the limited amount of +2 darts,
no armor, or their stats, still apply and influence their difficulty to
play them.

A note aside WRT peaceful mines; I got the impression that the mines
are never "peaceful", rather less hostile, depending on whether all
monsters there attack you, or whether gnomes, or whether dwarfs don't
attack (or rather; don't always attack) you. (Depending on alignment,
and/or race.)

> "Difficulty" is comprised of role, race, alignment, and environment.

Yes.

> Role alone is meaningless for judging difficulty.

Role alone is insufficient to judge, yes. But role is not meaningless
to judge, since there are relevant attributes associated with roles,
and those attributes (stats, equipment) influence the game to a non
negligible degree; especially in the beginning of the game, and also
especially for beginners.

Moreover, I think we also cannot exclude Nethack's possible race/role
combinations if someone asks the general - not the hypothetical -
question whether one role is more or less difficult. This just BTW.

